Here are **all the lines** from the provided text, as you asked to "pick as many lines as you can":

1. Take all my comfort of thy worth and truth.  
2. Serving with looks his sacred majesty;  
3. And yet methinks I have astronomy,  
4. Which I new pay as if not paid before.  
5. From sullen earth, sings hymns at heaven's gate;  
6. To find where your true image pictured lies;  
7. With what I most enjoy contented least;  
8. Cheered and cheque'd even by the self-same sky,

Here are as many lines as I could pick from the provided poem lines (I’ve avoided duplication unless it appeared twice in your input, in which case I've included it accordingly):

1. Then may I dare to boast how I do love thee;  
2. Stealing unseen to west with this disgrace:  
3. Neither in inward worth nor outward fair,  
4. To be death's conquest and make worms thine heir.  
5. Shifts but his place, for still the world enjoys it;  
6. Yet eyes this cunning want to grace their art;  
7. To find where your true image pictured lies
